// AUTO-GENERATED by gen:manual-index. Do not edit by hand.
import type { ChapterText } from "@/components/manual/chapters";

export const MANUAL_INDEX: ChapterText[] = [
	{"id":"introduction","title":"Introduction","text":"Map Making App is a desktop map editor for GeoGuessr. It's an alternative to map-making.app with the same editing model and mostly the same UI, but everything runs on your computer, and a lot is added on top. What's different? No account, no server. Every map lives on your computer. Built for large maps. Maps with millions of locations open and edit smoothly. Commits . Instead of one \"save\", Map Making App keeps a version history you can browse and go back to. Autosave . Uncommitted changes are kept, so you can close a map and pick up where you left off. Composable selections . Combine selections with AND, OR, and NOT. Filters . Select by metadata: country, image date, camera type, or any field. Bulk operations . Apply one change to a whole selection, e.g. setting a field or pinning panos. Metadata enrichment . Pull altitude, country, camera type, and capture dates onto your locations. Plugins . Extra panels and tools, including a location generator, plus a marketplace of downloadable ones. Seen history . Every panorama you've looked at, across all maps. Sync . Keep a map in step with one on map-making.app or GeoGuessr, or with a map file. Power-user touches. Rebindable hotkeys, a command palette , and custom CSS. Terms Location. One Street View spot on a map: coordinates, a view (heading, pitch, zoom), an optional pinned panorama, tags, and metadata. Panorama. A single Street View image. Each location loads one pano. Tag . A colored label you put on locations. A location either has it or doesn't. Metadata . Values on a location that aren't just on/off: a country code, an altitude, a capture date. Selection . A subset of locations, shown on the map in its own color. Commit . A saved version of the whole map. If you're new, start with Getting started ."},
	{"id":"first-run","title":"Getting started","text":"This chapter is about the basic flow for most users. It will be mostly familiar coming from the web version. This is a work in progress and data loss can rarely happen. Keep backups. The recommended backup is Export all maps on the home screen. Your first map Make a map. On the home screen, type a name in the search box and press Enter . Add a location. Click the map near Street View coverage. The location snaps to the nearest panorama and opens. Edit it. Look around until you have the view you want, then press Save ( Enter ). Tag it. Type a tag name in the bar under the panorama. New tags are created as you type them. Select it. Click a tag in the sidebar to select every location with that tag. Selections are what you review, export, and run bulk operations on. Commit. Your edits are saved automatically, but a commit (bottom bar, or Ctrl + S ) is a version of the map you can go back to. If your maps are on map-making.app, you can import them all at once . Everything is stored in the app's data folder on your computer."},
	{"id":"map-list","title":"Map list/home screen","text":"The home screen lists your maps. Click a map's name to open it in its own window. The buttons in the bottom-left open Discord, this manual, and the scratch map. The ones in the bottom-right import and export all your maps, and open plugins and settings. The home screen: searchable, sortable map list with folders. Search and create Search. Type to filter by map name or label. Every word has to match, and label:name (or label:\"two words\" ) only matches labels. Clicking a label on a row searches for it. Esc clears the search. Sort. By Name , Last opened , Date created , or Location count . New folder. Type a name, then press the folder button. New map. Type a name, then press + . Enter. Opens the map with that exact name, or creates and opens a new one. Map rows Drag handle. Drag a map onto a folder to move it there, or to the top to take it out. Edit. The pencil changes the map's name, description, and labels (keywords shown on the row that you can search by). Duplicate. Makes a copy, uncommitted changes included. The copy has no version history, sync links, or review sessions. Delete. The trash icon deletes the map, after asking. Which details each row shows (location count, dates, uncommitted changes, sync status) is set in Settings → Application → Map list . Folders Folders collapse and show their map and location totals. Deleting a folder moves its maps back out; it doesn't delete them. An empty folder disappears once you close the window. What's new The panel on the right lists recent releases. Versions newer than yours are marked, so you can see what an update brings. The scratch map The scratch map is a throwaway map for exploring locations without persisting them. It isn't in the list, has no history, and is cleared the next time Map Making App starts. To keep what you made, run Save as... from the command palette ."},
	{"id":"importing-existing","title":"Importing your existing maps","text":"If you use map-making.app , you can bring all your maps across at once. On the website, use Download data to get a ZIP of your maps. Then: On the Map Making App home screen, click Import maps (bottom-right). Choose the ZIP, or one or more JSON files. The preview lists each map with its location count, tag count, and folder. Pick which maps to import with All , None , or New only , or tick them one by one. Maps with the same name and location count as one you already have are marked duplicate and unticked, so importing the same ZIP twice doesn't double up. Press Import N maps . Progress shows in the bottom-right. Nothing is imported until you press the button. Export all maps , next to it, does the reverse: one ZIP of every map. It's also the recommended backup ."},
	{"id":"editor-layout","title":"Editor layout & the map","text":"Opening a map shows the map on the left and the sidebar on the right. Drag the divider between them to resize (it's remembered). The editor: map on one side, sidebar with Tags and Selections. The sidebar The header has: Back arrow. Back to the map list ( Ctrl + Shift + W ). Pencil. Opens Edit map : name, description, labels, and a few per-map settings . Database button. Opens Enrichment . Document button. Opens doclinks . Only there once a tag is linked to a doc. Below that are two sections: Tags . Every tag on the map, with a filter box, a folder view toggle, and sort buttons. Selections . Your active selections and their counts. The header has the plugin buttons and Commands... , which opens the command palette . Under the selections is a toolbar of shortcut buttons. Clicking a marker replaces the sidebar with the location editor . Moving around the map Pan / zoom. Drag and scroll, or W A S D to pan and Shift + W / Shift + S to zoom. Hold Alt to move slower. Shift + B fits all locations, Shift + E fits the selected ones, and Shift + 0 zooms all the way out. Place search. Type a place name to jump there. Pasting coordinates or a Google Maps link adds that location instead. Polygon tools. The buttons on the left draw polygon, rectangle, and freehand selections . The Map style button changes the basemap and layers , and Ctrl + \\ hides the sidebar for a fullscreen map ."},
	{"id":"map-layers","title":"Map style & layers","text":"The Map style button (top-left of the map) changes what's under your markers: the basemap, the Street View coverage lines, and borders. These apply to every map, not just the open one. The map style panel Basemap. Map , Satellite , OSM (OpenStreetMap), or Vector . J and K switch between them. Layers. Terrain , Labels , and Panoramas (Street View dots, when zoomed in close). The Street View coverage layer is always on. Street View. Which coverage lines to show ( Official , Unofficial , or All ), their color, thinner lines, and a blobby layer when zoomed out. Settings. Stronger country and subdivision borders, and switches to hide road labels, points of interest, transit, and highways. Map style. Default , Legacy (the classic GeoGuessr look), and a range of muted, dark, and high-contrast styles. Add your own with Manage map styles . Vector has its own style list and doesn't use the options above. Layer opacity The slider in the top-right fades one layer at a time. The buttons next to it pick which: Street View coverage, unselected markers, or selected markers (only there while something is selected). A button is crossed out while its layer is hidden. P hides and shows the Street View layer, and M the unselected markers."},
	{"id":"bottom-bar","title":"The bottom bar","text":"The bar along the bottom of the editor. The bottom bar: location count, Commit with diff, undo/redo, Seen, History, Import, Export. Location count. How many locations the map has. Commit . Saves your changes as a version. Next to it, +added , -removed , and ±modified count what's changed since the last commit. Shift +click to add a message. Undo / Redo . Step back and forward through your edits ( Ctrl + Z / Ctrl + Y ). Seen . Every panorama you've looked at. History . This map's commits. Import file . Adds locations from a file. Export . Saves the map, or the selection, to a file."},
	{"id":"command-palette","title":"Command palette & toolbar","text":"The command palette ( Ctrl + K , or Commands... in the sidebar) lists every command. Type part of its name and press Enter . The command palette: type to search every command, with its shortcut beside it. The palette Search. Matching is loose, and some commands answer to other words too (\"save\" finds Commit). Groups. Map , Bulk Operations , Selections , Tags , and Plugins . Shortcuts. A command's key binding shows next to it. Any command can be given one in Settings → Keyboard . Greyed out. Commands that can't run right now (Undo with nothing to undo) are greyed out, not hidden. Open map... jumps to another map. The toolbar The row of buttons under your selections is a toolbar of commands. It starts with some selection and review commands, and you can change it: Add. Click the pin next to a command in the palette, or right-click it. Rearrange. Drag buttons around, or right-click one to move it, add a separator, or remove it. Bulk-add tag. The box at the end tags every selected location. Type to find a tag, or press Enter to make a new one. A few commands (find duplicates, filter by metadata, top/bottom K, and the pickers) open a small form under the toolbar instead of a dialog, so the map stays visible."},
	{"id":"map-context-menu","title":"Map context menu","text":"Right-click the map for this menu. Right-clicking a location adds actions for that location at the top. On a location Copy Street View link. A Google Maps link with the location's view and tags. Shortened when Google's shortener answers. Copy pano ID. Download panorama. Saves the full panorama image. Copy to map... Copies the location into another map. Duplicate location. Adds a copy at the same spot. Delete location. Anywhere on the map Start measurement / End measurement. Measures distance from the clicked point. Copy coordinates. Copies the point as lat, lng . Select this country / Select this subdivision. Selects everything inside that border. Subdivisions need Subdivision data turned on in Settings → Map → Borders . Delete this polygon. Removes the polygon selection under the point. Set / Clear latitude/longitude anchors. Lines through the point, for lining things up. Faster ways to do the border and polygon items: hold Q and click to select a country ( Q + Shift for a subdivision), and hold E and click a polygon to remove it."},
	{"id":"fullscreen","title":"Fullscreen modes","text":"There are two fullscreen modes: the panorama ( F ) and the map ( Ctrl + \\ ). Only one can be on at a time, and Esc leaves either. Fullscreen panorama With a location open, F fills the window with the panorama. A minimap and tag bar sit on top, so you can keep tagging and placing locations. Hover the minimap to make it bigger. Settings → Street View → Fullscreen panorama sets what shows: the minimap, tag bar, date picker, review bar, and address. Fullscreen map Ctrl + \\ hides the sidebar so the map fills the window. An open location shows as a small preview in the corner. It has the panorama but no buttons, so use Enter to save, Esc to close, and Delete to delete. Settings → Map → Fullscreen map sets whether the bottom bar and the preview show."},
	{"id":"adding-locations","title":"Adding locations","text":"Most of the time you add a location by clicking the map. You can also add one from the panorama you're in, or paste it in. Whole files go through Import file . Click the map Click anywhere and Map Making App doesn't just drop a pin there. It finds the nearest Street View coverage, puts the location on it, and opens it. Which panorama it picks follows the map's coverage settings . If there's no coverage nearby, nothing is added. If you only want to look around, Click behavior in Map settings can stop clicks from adding locations ( O toggles this) or make a click open the nearest location instead. From the panorama With a location open: Drop marker ( C ) adds a new location where you're standing, with your current view. Walk down the road, press C , keep walking. Follow road ( G ) walks along the road in the direction you're facing and adds a location at each panorama. Paste Paste anywhere in the editor (outside a text box): A Google Maps link or coordinates add that location and open it. Short links ( maps.app.goo.gl ) work too. Several links, one per line , or JSON/CSV text, open the import preview first. Pasting into the place search box works the same way. Crosshair Hold Enter to show a crosshair in the middle of the map, then let go to click there. It only works with no location open, since Enter saves the open one. It can be a bit janky, but it's there if you want to go mouseless."},
	{"id":"click-coverage","title":"How a click picks coverage","text":"When you click the map, there's often more than one panorama nearby: official and unofficial, old and new cameras, both directions of a road. The Selecting new locations settings in the Map settings dropdown decide which one you get and which way it faces. They're saved per map. The Map settings dropdown: Selecting new locations at the top, then the view preferences. Point view along the road by default. New locations face along the road. Direction picks which way: forwards, backwards, most northern/eastern/southern/western, or random. Prefer official coverage over unofficial. Prefer higher quality over newer images. Take the better camera over the most recent capture. Disallow unofficial coverage. Never pick unofficial coverage, even if it's the only thing there. Use Pano ID locations by default. Pin every new location to its exact panorama. Min search radius. How far from your click to look for coverage, from 10m to 500m (default 50m). In dense areas, a smaller radius helps you get the exact pano you clicked on. Show click search radius at cursor draws it. To start new maps with the same settings, set them on one map and use Use current map in Settings → Map → Defaults ."},
	{"id":"location-editor","title":"Location editor & pano viewer","text":"Click a marker to open it in the location editor: a live Street View panorama, with the location's details and tags underneath. The location editor: live panorama, date picker, Save/Close/Delete, and the tag bar. Editing and saving Look around until you have the view you want, then press Save (or Enter ). Save keeps the heading, pitch, zoom, panorama, coordinates, and tag changes. Save. Saves and closes (or, in a review , moves to the next location). Close. Closes without saving ( Esc ). Delete. Deletes the location ( Delete ). Nothing changes until you press Save. Close throws your changes away. Panned vs unpanned A location is unpanned until you've saved a view for it (its heading is still zero). The Unpanned selection finds them, which pairs well with a review. Country and address Under the panorama is a flag and an address. Where the address comes from is set in Settings → Editing → Geocoding : Local (offline, city and region), Nominatim (street address from OpenStreetMap), or Google (what the panorama says about itself). Tags and metadata The bar under the address adds and removes tags . If the map has Enrich locations turned on, metadata like camera type and capture date is filled in as you look, and saved with the location. Locations opened from the seen history or an import preview aren't on the map yet. For a seen one, Save reads Add to map ."},
	{"id":"pano-controls","title":"Pano controls & movement","text":"The controls on the panorama for looking around and moving. Each one can be turned off in Settings → Street View → Viewer controls . The rest of the overlay ( links, screenshots, and display options ) has its own chapter. The panorama overlay: compass, zoom, return-to-spawn, jump, coordinates, and map links. Looking around Compass. Turns with your view. Click it to face north, or Ctrl -click to turn toward each road. Face north ( N ). Press it again while facing north to look straight down. Turn around ( T ), or face along the road ( B ). Zoom. + / - , and 0 to zoom all the way out. Arrow keys look around. Moving Walk. Click the arrows on the road, or Shift + Up / Shift + Down . Jump forward / backward. About 100m along the road ( } / { ). Return to spawn ( R ). Back to where the location started, with its saved view. This also unpins the pano. Shift + R just reloads the panorama. Center the map ( L ) on where you're standing, handy once you've walked away from the marker. Walking doesn't move the location until you save. To keep it and add a new one where you're standing, use Drop marker ( C ). Movement modes and speed Movement mode. Moving (walk and look), No Move (look only), or NMPZ (no moving, panning, or zooming). Shift + M switches between them, so you can check a location the way it'll be played. The starting mode is set in Settings → Street View . Speed. Look speed is in Settings → Street View , map pan speed in Settings → Map → Navigation . Hold Alt to slow both down."},
	{"id":"pano-tools","title":"Pano links, screenshots & display","text":"The panorama also has buttons for copying links, taking screenshots, and changing how it looks. Like the other pano controls , each can be turned off in Settings → Street View → Viewer controls . Links and images Map links. Open the view in Google Maps, or copy a link to it ( Ctrl + C ). Hold Shift to leave the tags out, or Alt for the long URL instead of a short one. Screenshot. Copies what you're looking at as a 1920x1080 image. Hold Shift to save it as a file instead. Download panorama ( Ctrl + Shift + S ). Saves the whole panorama, not just your view. For many at once, use Download panoramas . Info Altitude and zoom. Shown in the corner. Pano metadata. An optional readout of whether the location is pinned, plus the metadata a save would write. Handy while enriching or checking a location. Display Crosshair ( X ) in the middle of the view. Hide car ( Ctrl + H ). Hide pano UI ( H ) for a clean view. By default this hides the road arrows too. Aspect ratio. Shape the panorama like the game will (4:3 up to 32:9), or Free to fill the space. Set in Settings → Street View . Lock view ( V ). Keeps your view the same relative to the car as you move between panoramas, instead of resetting at each one. F opens the fullscreen panorama ."},
	{"id":"coverage-dates","title":"Coverage dates & camera types","text":"Most places have been photographed more than once. The date picker under the panorama lists every capture of the pano you're looking at, so you can choose which one the location shows. Pick a date to load that capture. [ and ] step through them. Default loads whatever the coordinates show, and keeps up as new coverage comes out. Camera badges show each capture's camera generation. Turn them off in Settings → Street View → Date picker . Unofficial coverage is always marked. If every capture of a pano is unofficial, the official captures right next to it are listed too. More on camera generations in this guide . Pinning a date Picking a date pins that exact pano to the location. Default or Return to spawn unpins it again. Once a location has an exact date , the picker shows that instead of just the month."},
	{"id":"loading-flags","title":"Loading mode & flags","text":"A location loads its panorama in one of two ways: By coordinates. Whatever the default coverage is at that point. This is the lighter option, and right when the default coverage is what you want. By pano ID (Load as pano ID). Pinned to one exact panorama. Needed for a specific date, an unofficial pano, or anything the coordinates alone wouldn't load. Map Making App pins a location for you when you pick a specific date , or when a click lands on a pano that isn't the default there. Use Pano ID locations by default in the map's coverage settings pins every new location. The trade-off: a coordinate location moves on to newer coverage when Google updates the area, while a pinned one stays exactly as it is - until that pano is taken down or changed. If a pinned pano can't be found, the editor tells you and falls back to the coordinates. To pin or unpin many at once, use Pin locations to pano ID from bulk operations . The Pano ID locations and Coordinate locations selections find locations by how they load, which is useful for checking a map before export. Validation checks whether pinned panos still exist."},
	{"id":"reviewing","title":"Reviewing","text":"Review mode goes through a selection one location at a time, and keeps track of which ones you've already looked at. You can stop and pick up where you left off. Review mode Start it with Review selection on a selection 's menu, or Review selected locations in the command palette (also on the toolbar). Next / Previous. Ctrl + Right and Ctrl + Left . Save also moves on and marks the location reviewed. Skip. Esc moves on without saving, and still marks it reviewed. Delete. Deletes the location and moves on. Exit review. The X in the review bar. Your progress is kept. The bar shows where you are ( 3 / 50 ) and how many you've reviewed. Clicking a marker that's in the review jumps there. Clicking one that isn't just opens it without losing your place. Picking up where you left off Running Review selection on the same selection again continues the review instead of starting over. Review sessions lists all of them. The review sessions dialog: in-progress and completed reviews with progress bars. In progress / Completed. Two tabs. Resume. Continue from where you stopped. Select reviewed / Select unreviewed. The two circle buttons select the locations you have or haven't reviewed yet. Rename by clicking the name, or Delete . Select reviewed locations in the command palette selects everything you've ever reviewed on the map, across all sessions. While reviewing, the Reviewed and Unreviewed selections show your progress on the map. Review order By default a review goes in the order the selection lists them. To see the most important ones first, set a Review order in Edit map (the pencil by the map name). It's a small formula that scores each location, highest first. tagCount , for example, puts the most-tagged locations first."},
	{"id":"duplicates","title":"Duplicates & deleting","text":"Maps pick up duplicates: the same pano added twice, two locations a few meters apart, an import that overlaps what you had. There are tools for each. Several locations at one spot Clicking a marker with other locations within two meters of it doesn't open the panorama. The sidebar lists everything at that spot instead, each with a thumbnail and its tags. Click a thumbnail to open that location. Delete on a row deletes just that one. Tick rows, then Keep selected (deletes the others) or Delete selected . This is the way out of accidentally adding the same pano twice. Across the whole map Find duplicates... selects every location with another one within a distance you choose. From there, two tools that are easy to mix up: Merge duplicates... collapses each group into one location, and puts all their tags on the one that's left. Works on the whole map. Prune duplicates (on the Duplicates selection's menu) deletes the extras instead, and only touches that selection. Under 25m it keeps the best location in each group; above that it thins them out to that spacing. Use Merge when the duplicates have tags you want to keep, and Prune when you just want them gone. Both keep whichever location scores highest on the map's Duplicate preference (in Edit map ). By default that's the one with the most tags, a pinned pano, and a saved view; ties go to the oldest. You can write your own as an expression . Duplicating on purpose Duplicate location in the right-click menu adds a copy at the same spot. To add another view further down the road, use Drop marker instead. Deleting One. Delete or Delete in the location editor. Many. Select them, then Delete on the map, or Delete selected locations from the command palette or toolbar. Ctrl + Z brings deleted locations back."},
	{"id":"tags","title":"Tags","text":"Tags are colored labels you put on locations. A location either has a tag or it doesn't. For values like a date or an altitude, use metadata instead. Adding tags Tag bar. In the location editor, type a name and pick a suggestion, or press Enter to make a new tag. The X on a tag takes it off. Quicktags. With a location open, 1 through 9 toggle the first nine tags in the list. These keys can be rebound. Tag hotkeys. Assign a key in a tag's edit dialog. Give several tags the same key to toggle them together: a press adds any missing tags, or removes the group if all are already on the location. These are per map . Many at once. Select locations and type a tag into Bulk-add tag at the end of the toolbar. Save as tag on a selection's menu does the same. Click a tag in the sidebar to select every location with it, and click again to deselect. Shift-click selects a range. Colors, order, and visibility Color. Set in the tag's edit dialog. To recolor several, select them, right-click one, and choose Recolor tags . Order. default is your own order (drag tags to change it), or sort by name or amount . Quicktags follow the same order. Filter. The box at the top narrows the list. Visibility. A tag only shows while some location has it. Suggestions and spacing. How many tags the tag bar suggests, and the gap between tag pills, are in Settings → Editing → Tags . Tags with a / in the name ( Europe/France ) can be shown as folders . Tag tools Right-click a tag for: Remove from all. Takes the tag off every location. If several tags are selected, it takes all of them. Remove from selection only touches the selected locations. Rename in selection. Gives only the selected locations a different tag name, splitting the tag. If the name already exists, they merge into it. Recolor tags. In the command palette ( Tags group): Apply metadata as tags . One tag per value of a field, e.g. a tag per country. Find and replace in tag names. Download tag counts as CSV. Assign document links . Link tags to sections of a Google Doc. Tag changes can be undone like any other edit."},
	{"id":"tag-folders","title":"Tag folders","text":"Tags with a / in their name can be shown as folders: Europe/France goes under Europe . Turn it on with the tree button in the Tags header (or View mode in Settings → Editing → Tags ). Using folders Clicking a folder selects every tag in it. Alt -click selects just the folder's own tag. The arrow opens and closes a folder. Ctrl -click it to open or close everything inside. Folder color is gray by default, or can match the first tag inside ( Folder color in the same settings). Short names. Truncate tag names to shortest unique path hides the repeated part of long nested names. Organizing Drag a tag onto a folder to move it in. That renames it ( France becomes Europe/France ). Drop it on Move to top level to take it back out. New folder in the header makes a folder that isn't a tag itself. The pencil on a tag or folder edits only that one. To rename or recolor a folder and everything in it, right-click it and use Rename folder or Recolor tags . Add alias (right-click a tag) shows the tag in a second place in the tree too. Remove from all on a folder takes off every tag inside it. Remove this tag only leaves the tags inside alone."},
	{"id":"selections","title":"Selections","text":"A selection is a group of your locations, highlighted on the map in its own color. The Selections section lists the active ones with how many locations they match. They update as you edit. Anything that works on many locations at once (reviewing, exporting, bulk operations, deleting) works on what's selected. The Selections section: each rule drawn in its own color, with a per-selection menu. Kinds of selections Tag and Untagged. Locations with a tag, or with none. Click a tag in the sidebar to select it. Unpanned. Locations you haven't saved a view for yet. Pano ID locations / Coordinate locations. By how they load . Uncommitted. Added or changed since the last commit. Polygon. An area you draw with the buttons on the left of the map: polygon, rectangle, or freehand. Click the first point or double-click to finish; Esc cancels. Hold E and click a polygon to remove it. Country / Subdivision. Hold Q and click the map for a country, Q + Shift for a state or province. Subdivisions need Subdivision data turned on in Settings → Map → Borders . Duplicates . Locations within a distance of each other. Validation . Problems found by validation. Reviewed / Unreviewed . The two halves of a review. Filter . By a field's value, e.g. altitude above 500m. Top / Bottom K. The highest or lowest K locations by a field. Manual. Locations you Ctrl -click one by one. Everything. Ctrl + A . Ctrl + D clears all selections. GeoJSON shapes. Load shapes from GeoJSON as selection turns shapes from a .geojson file into polygons. Selections can be combined , narrowed to a random or spread-out sample , and saved for other maps. The selection menu Click a selection's name to zoom to it. The dots button has: Invert selection. Everything except this. Edit filter. For filter selections. Review selection. Go through it in a review . Save as tag. Puts a new tag on everything in it. Prune duplicates. On a duplicates selection, deletes the extras. Change color. Tag selections use the tag's color instead. Download GeoJSON and Rename. For polygons. Deselect. To tag everything selected, type into Bulk-add tag at the end of the toolbar. Ghosting The ghost button on a selection dims it on the map without removing it. Alt -click it to ghost every other selection instead, and Alt -click again to bring them back. A ghosted selection is left out of anything that acts on \"the selection\" (delete, bulk operations, Bulk-add tag), so you can keep it around without it getting in the way. Undoing selection changes Selections have their own undo, separate from your edits: Ctrl + Alt + D , and add Shift to redo. It lasts while the map is open."},
	{"id":"sampling","title":"Sampling a selection","text":"Three commands pick a smaller set out of the current selection: a random sample, or one that's spread out evenly. Each one replaces the selection with what it picked. Run them from the command palette or the toolbar. Random Pick random locations from selection picks that many at random. Asking for more than the selection has just keeps all of it. Handy for cutting a big map down to a set you can review or export. Thinning Thin selection by minimum distance keeps a spread-out part of the selection. Count keeps that many, as far apart as possible. Min distance keeps as many as possible while staying at least that far apart. The distance is only a minimum: neighbors can end up anywhere from that to more than twice as far apart. Evenly spaced Pick evenly spaced locations from selection lays a honeycomb over the selection and keeps the location closest to the middle of each cell, so the picks are about the same distance apart. Set Spacing , or a Count to spread that many as widely as possible. No two picks are ever closer than half the spacing. This works best over an area; along a single road the gaps vary more. From each selection With two or more selections active, all three can pick from each of N selections . The count is then per selection: with eight tags selected and a count of 25, you get up to 25 from each tag. A location in two of them is only picked once, so you might get fewer. This is how you make an evenly weighted map out of tags with very different sizes. It works with any selection, not just tags."},
	{"id":"selection-algebra","title":"Selection algebra","text":"Selections can be combined with AND, OR, and NOT, so you can ask for things like \"tagged France, inside this polygon, and not panned yet\" without writing any code. Combining selections: intersection (AND), union (OR), and invert (NOT). Operations Intersection (AND). Locations in every one of them. Command: Intersect (AND) selections . Union (OR). Locations in any of them. Command: Union (OR) selections . Invert (NOT). Everything except a selection. On a selection's menu, or Invert selection in the command palette for all of them at once. The commands combine every active selection that isn't ghosted . Drag to combine Drag one selection onto another in the sidebar: a plain drag makes an AND, and holding Alt makes an OR. The combined selection shows the others indented under it. Dropping on the top or bottom edge of a selection reorders instead. Deselect on a combined selection splits it back up. Example To find locations tagged France , inside a polygon you drew, but not panned yet: add the Tag, Polygon, and Unpanned selections, AND the tag with the polygon, then AND that with the inverted Unpanned."},
	{"id":"filters","title":"Filters","text":"A filter selects locations by a field's value, e.g. everything above 500m altitude, or everything from 2023. Make one with Filter by metadata... in the command palette or toolbar. To change it later, use Edit filter on its menu. The filter form: pick a field, an operator, and a value. Filters only see metadata that's already on your locations, so enrich first. Fields Built-in. Created , Modified , Pano ID , Load as pano ID , Tags , Tag count , Heading , Pitch , and Zoom . Metadata. Any field on the map, like Country code , Camera type , Altitude , Image date , and your own fields. What you can ask depends on the field's type . Operators = / != equals and not equals. > / < / >= / <= for numbers and dates. between a range. Date and month fields can tick Any year , e.g. June to August in any year. Exact dates can tick Any date to match a time of day instead. has / does not have a value at all. contains / does not contain for lists like tags, plus comparisons on their length. Fields with a fixed set of values (like camera type) only have the equals operators, with a list to pick from. Dates and timezones Dates are compared in your own timezone by default. Tick Location timezone on an exact-date filter to use each location's local time instead, which matters when filtering by time of day across countries. Locations with no timezone are skipped, and you're told how many. Exact-date filters pick whole days, matching how dates are shown. With Exact date format set to Date + time ( Settings → Street View → Date picker ), they get a time box too. Stepping through ranges Filter selections with a range have left/right arrows. They move the range forward or back by its own size, so you can step through dates day by day, or altitudes band by band. To ask for more than one thing at once (Gen 4 and above 500m), combine filters ."},
	{"id":"saved-selections","title":"Saved selections","text":"You can save the selections you have active and apply them to other maps. Build a useful set of filters once, and reuse it everywhere. Both are in the command palette: Save current selections... Names your active selections and saves them. Apply saved selection... Adds a saved set to the open map, next to whatever's already selected. You can delete old sets here too. What carries over Tags are saved by name (not case-sensitive), so they work on other maps. Polygons, filters, untagged, unpanned, pano ID, uncommitted, top/bottom K, duplicates, and combined selections all carry over. Some can't be saved, because they only mean something on one map: Manual , Validation , and review progress. If part of a saved set doesn't fit the map you apply it to (like a tag that doesn't exist there), that part just matches nothing. The rest still applies."},
	{"id":"bulk-operations","title":"Bulk operations","text":"Bulk operations run one action on many locations at once. They're in the Bulk Operations group of the command palette. Each one asks whether to run on All locations or the Current selection (the default when something is selected), and shows progress as it runs. The operations Validate locations . Checks coverage and reports problems. Enrich metadata fields . Fetches metadata. Set metadata field value. Writes one value into a field. Besides your own fields, you can set Heading , Pitch , and Zoom (the same view everywhere), and Load as pano ID . Number fields also take an expression , like mod(heading + 180, 360) to turn every view around. Clear metadata fields. Removes fields. Pan headings along road. Points each view along the road, forwards or backwards . Pin locations to pano ID. Pins each location to a pano. You can choose which capture, and Unpin does the reverse. Download panoramas. Saves an image of each: the full panorama, the view, a thumbnail, or a tile. Several come as a ZIP. Full panoramas are slow and large, so pick the scope carefully. While it runs Closing the dialog doesn't stop it. Running jobs show in the bottom-right, where you can open or cancel them. If some locations fail, Select failed selects them so you can retry. The whole run is one undo. An edit that changes more than 500,000 locations clears the undo history to save memory. Commit first if you might want to go back."},
	{"id":"enrichment","title":"Metadata enrichment","text":"Enrichment looks up information about each location's panorama (altitude, country, camera, capture date, and so on) and saves it on the location as metadata. Unlike tags, metadata can be a number, a date, or text, so you can filter and sort by it. Fields Five are on by default: Altitude. Height of the pano in meters. Country code. Which country the coverage is in. Camera type. Gen 1, Gen 2/3, Gen 4, tripod, trekker, or bad cam. Pano type. Official, unknown, or user-uploaded. Image date. Year and month of capture. Six more you can turn on: Exact date. The exact capture time. Much slower than the rest (several requests per location). How it's shown is set in Settings → Street View → Date picker . Timezone. The pano's timezone (e.g. America/New_York ). Use it with exact date to filter by local time of day. Driving direction. Which way the car was facing, in degrees. Uploader. Who uploaded the panorama. Coverage dates. Every capture date at that spot. Subdivision. The state, province, or region. Worked out offline from border data (downloaded the first time), so it can differ slightly from other sources near borders. Some fields need others: Timezone needs Exact date, which needs Image date. Turning one on keeps the ones it needs on too. Plugins can add more fields. The Enrichment dialog Open it with the database button next to the map name, or Open enrichment in the command palette. It has two tabs: Enrich. The Enrich locations switch, and a switch per field. Each field shows how much of the map has it already. Fields . What each field is called and what type it is. These settings are per map. The Enrich tab: which fields to fetch, and how much of the map already has each one. Running it As you go. With Enrich locations on, each location you open is enriched, and the metadata is saved when you press Save. It's off for new maps. All at once. Run Enrich metadata fields from the command palette on the whole map or the selection. Locations that already have the metadata are skipped, unless you tick Re-enrich already enriched locations . It's a bulk operation , so it runs in the background. What to do with it Filter by it . E.g. everything from 2023, or Gen 4 above 500m. Turn it into tags . A tag per country, per year, or per 500m of altitude. Color by it . The Gradient plugin colors locations by any number or date field."},
	{"id":"managing-fields","title":"Managing metadata fields","text":"Every metadata field has a definition: its name, its type, and how it's compared. Enriched fields come set up already. Fields from an imported file, or ones you make yourself, might need fixing. Edit them in the Fields tab of the Enrichment dialog (the database button next to the map name). The Fields tab: every field on the map, and the definition of the one you picked. Pick a field on the left to edit it: Label. The name shown in pickers and filters. Type. Text , Number , True/false , Date/time , Month (YYYY-MM) , Enum , or Array . An enum is a fixed list of values; you can fill the list from what's already on your locations. Compare as. How Disambiguate compares values: Auto , Numeric , Circular (values that wrap around, like 360 degrees or 24 hours), or Categorical . Rename. Change the field's key. Renaming to a key that already exists merges the two, and you choose whose values win where both have one. Delete field. Removes the field from every location. Each field has a bar showing how many locations have a value. Changes apply right away (there's no Save button), and rename, merge, and delete can be undone. The type decides which operators a filter offers. If a filter is offering the wrong ones, the field probably has the wrong type."},
	{"id":"metadata-as-tags","title":"Metadata as tags","text":"Apply metadata as tags (command palette, Tags group) makes one tag per value of a field and puts each location in its tag. Applying Country code , for example, gives you a tag per country in one step. Subdivision does the same per state or province. Grouping values A field with thousands of different values would make thousands of tags, so you can group them first: Dates by year, year-month, day, month of year, or hour of day. Tick Location timezone to use each location's local time. Months by year or by month of year. Numbers in fixed-size ranges, e.g. altitude in 500m bands. Lists get a tag for every item. The dialog shows how many tags and locations you'll get before you apply it, and warns past 100 tags. Naming Tag name. {value} is the value and {field} the field's name. A / makes a folder : Country/{value} puts every country under one folder. No value. Tick it to also tag locations that don't have the field. Scope. All locations or just the selection. The Gradient plugin groups values the same ways, for coloring instead of tagging."},
	{"id":"expressions","title":"Expressions","text":"A few places take a small formula instead of a fixed value. It's the same formula language in all of them: Set metadata field value works out a value for each location. Duplicate preference scores which duplicate is kept. Review order scores which locations a review shows first. The last two are in Edit map (the pencil by the map name). Writing one An expression reads a location's fields and does math with them. Fields. Use the field's key: heading , altitude , lat , tagCount , or any of your own. Math. + - * / % and brackets. Comparisons. > < >= <= == != . True counts as 1 and false as 0, so you can add them to a score. Functions. mod , clamp , abs , round , floor , min , max , if(condition, then, else) , and has(field) for whether a location has a value. Examples mod(heading + 180, 360) turns every view around. clamp(pitch, -10, 10) keeps views close to level. tagCount + has(panoId) prefers well-tagged, pinned locations. if(altitude > 1000, 2, 0) + tagCount puts mountains first, then the most-tagged. The default duplicate preference is tagCount + has(panoId) + loadAsPanoId + (heading != 0) : a point each for every tag, having a pano, being pinned, and having a saved view."},
	{"id":"doclinks","title":"Doclinks","text":"A doclink ties a tag to a heading in a Google Doc. Select that tag and the relevant section of the doc appears in a panel beside the map. It's for the common case of a meta guide living in a doc while the map lives here: instead of alt-tabbing to find what Bollards/Czechia looks like, you select the tag and read it in place. Assigning links Run Assign document links... from the command palette (Tags group) and paste the doc's link. Map Making App loads every heading in the doc. Click a tag, then click headings to link or unlink it. A tag can link to several headings, and a heading can serve several tags. The tag list matches your sidebar (same folders and order), and both sides have a filter box. If the map links more than one doc, buttons under the link switch between them. Match names suggests links wherever a heading has the same name as a tag (ignoring case and accents). A nested tag like Bollards/Czechia also matches a heading called just Czechia , as long as no other tag ends that way (a Poles/Czechia tag would make it ambiguous). Dismiss any you don't want, then Link all . The doc has to be viewable by anyone with the link, since Map Making App reads it without signing in. Reading them Once a tag has a doclink, a document button appears above the sidebar. Open the panel and it follows your selection: select a linked tag and its section shows. The panel header has: Refresh. Reloads the doc after you've edited it. Whole document. Shows the full doc, scrolled to the heading. Pin. Stops following your selection, so the section stays put. Open in browser. If a tag links to more than one heading, numbered buttons switch between them. Drag the panel's edge to resize it. Google Maps links in the doc work too: clicking one selects that location if it's on the map, or adds it if it isn't. Doclinks are kept when you export in the Local format. To switch the map to a different doc, clear the old one first with Clear doc links , next to the link in the assign dialog."},
	{"id":"validation","title":"Validation","text":"Validation checks whether each location still has Street View coverage, and whether newer or better coverage exists. Coverage changes over time: panos get taken down and new ones come out. Run Validate locations from the command palette on the whole map or the selection. It only reports. It never changes your locations. Choosing which findings to report before running validation. What it reports You choose which results you want before it runs. Each one becomes its own selection, and a location can be in more than one. The default pano is the one a location's coordinates load when it isn't pinned. Valid location. Nothing wrong. Newer coverage available. A pinned location, and newer official coverage exists. Coverage updated since last view. An unpinned location, and its stored pano is older than what's there now. Not found. No coverage at the coordinates or pano ID. Pano ID broke. The pinned pano no longer exists. Unofficial. User-uploaded coverage. Badcam, but good coverage available. A bad camera, but a better one exists at that spot. Off by default: Newer coverage than the stored pano. Either of the two newer-coverage results, pinned or not. Pinned away from the default pano. Pinned to a pano that isn't the default. Default pano is not the newest. The coordinates load an older capture, though a newer one exists at that pano. What to do with results The results are selections like any other. For example, review every Pano ID broke location and re-pin it."},
	{"id":"seen","title":"Seen history","text":"Map Making App keeps a history of every panorama you look at, across all maps. It's useful for going back to somewhere you've been, and for seeing where you've already looked. Recording It's on by default. Settings → Editing → Seen turns it off, and sets whether thumbnails are saved and at what size (bigger thumbnails take more space). Browsing Seen in the bottom bar opens the history: a thumbnail, flag, and address for each visit. Click one to open it. You can filter by country, by map, or by searching the address. It keeps the most recent 10,000, dropping the oldest. Clear wipes it. On the map Toggle seen locations overlay (command palette) draws every pano you've seen as a dot on the map. Blue dots are already on this map, and clicking opens the location. Orange dots are only in your history, and clicking previews the pano with the option to add it. Nothing is added until you say so. It's the quick way to see where you've already been while filling in an area: the orange dots are the ones you looked at and didn't keep."},
	{"id":"scoring","title":"Scoring, measurement & 5K radius","text":"GeoGuessr scores a guess by how far it is from the location, compared to the size of the map. A map's score bounds set that size, which decides how harshly distance is punished. Set them in Edit map (the pencil by the map name), under Scoring . Score bounds in Edit map: automatic, world map, or fixed. Automatic based on locations. From the area your locations cover. Updates as you add or remove them. World map (ACW). Scored like a world map. Fixed bounds. A box you type in as south, west, north, and east. Measuring distance Right-click the map and choose Start measurement . Click to add points, or drag a point to move it. Along with the distance, it shows the score that distance would get on this map. Esc or End measurement stops. The 5K radius Display 5K radius (in the Map settings dropdown, on by default) draws a circle around the open location: a guess anywhere inside scores 5000. Its size comes from the score bounds (at least 25m), and it follows you as you walk away from the marker. Distances follow Units in Settings → Application ."},
	{"id":"saving","title":"Saving, autosave & undo","text":"There's no \"save file\" in Map Making App. Your work is kept in a few layers: Save in the location editor keeps your changes to the open location. Until then you can throw them away with Close. Autosave writes every edit to disk shortly after you stop. Nothing is lost if you close the app. Undo / Redo step back and forward through your edits ( Ctrl + Z / Ctrl + Y ). Commit saves the whole map as a version you can go back to. Use undo for \"oops\", use commits for milestones. Autosave Autosave isn't a commit, it's a safety net. Reopen a map and your uncommitted changes are there exactly as you left them, undo history included, even after a crash. With Restore open maps on startup on ( Settings → Application → Startup , on by default), the maps you had open come back when you start Map Making App again. Undo Each edit is one undo, however many locations it touched: a bulk operation, an import, or a merge all undo in one go. Committing clears the undo history, since the commit is now how you go back. Selections have their own separate undo ( Ctrl + Alt + D ), so changing what's selected never gets in the way of undoing an edit. An edit that changes more than 500,000 locations clears the undo history to save memory. Commit before anything that big."},
	{"id":"commits","title":"Commits & diffs","text":"A Commit saves your current changes as a version of the map. Unlike autosave, it's a checkpoint you can come back to. The Commit button. In the bottom bar, greyed out when there's nothing to commit. A click (or Ctrl + S ) commits right away. Shift +click first shows what's changed and lets you add a message , which labels the commit in the history. The changes. Next to the button: +added , -removed , and ±modified since the last commit. The Uncommitted selection shows which locations those are. Committing clears the undo history. Past commits are in History in the bottom bar."},
	{"id":"version-history","title":"Version history & reverting","text":"History in the bottom bar lists the map's commits , newest first. Columns. Date, a short ID, the message and changes (+added / -removed / ±modified), and how many locations the map had. See what changed. Click a row to show that commit's changes on the map: added locations in green, removed in red, changed in amber. Revert / Restore. The newest commit has Revert ; older ones have Restore . Either one makes a new commit that puts the map back the way it was. It asks first. Restoring doesn't erase anything: the later commits stay in the list, so you can restore forward again. Uncommitted changes are dropped, though, so commit them first if you want them."},
	{"id":"bulk-backup","title":"Bulk backup & restore","text":"The two buttons in the bottom-right of the home screen move your whole library in and out. Export all maps. One ZIP of every map, named with today's date. This is the recommended backup. Import maps. Restores from that ZIP, or imports JSON files, the same way as importing from map-making.app . Make a backup before anything risky (bulk deletes, big imports, moving your data folder). Restoring is just importing the ZIP again. What's in the ZIP Every map as it is right now, uncommitted changes included: locations, tags, and metadata. It does not include version history, folders, or per-map settings. Other ways to back up Use Git on your data folder . This keeps everything, history included. Point your data folder at a network or synced drive ( Settings → Application → Data → Change folder ). Sync can keep a map on map-making.app or GeoGuessr up to date, but that's for keeping a copy current, not a backup."},
	{"id":"importing-into-map","title":"Importing into a map","text":"Import file in the bottom bar adds locations from a file to the open map. You can also drag a JSON or CSV file onto the editor, or paste a list of links. Either way you get a preview first, with the new locations shown on the map in their own color. To import whole maps instead, use Import maps on the home screen. The import panel Location count. How many locations the file has. Tags in file. The tags it found. Fields. Every metadata field in the file, with how many locations have it. Untick a field to leave it out (remembered for next time). Tag all imported locations. Adds one or more tags to everything you import, so you can find the batch later. Warnings. Anything that went wrong reading the file. Press Import to add them, or Discard to cancel. Formats JSON and CSV are both detected automatically, and common column names ( lat / latitude , lng / lon / longitude , pano / panoId ) all work, so files from other tools usually just import. The import doesn't check for locations you already have, so an overlapping file can leave duplicates . Imports of more than 500,000 locations are committed straight away and can't be undone (you can still revert them from the history). You're asked first."},
	{"id":"exporting-map","title":"Exporting a map","text":"Export in the bottom bar saves the map, or just the selection, to a file. The export dialog: scope, JSON format and options, then CSV and GeoJSON. JSON (recommended) Copy it or download it. Format picks how much goes in, depending on where it's going: GeoGuessr. Coordinates, views, and pinned pano IDs. The smallest file, for uploading. map-making.app. Adds tags, unpinned pano IDs, and capture months. No custom fields. Local. Everything, including custom fields and doclinks. Use this for moving a map between Map Making App installs. Options: Save zoom levels. Include each location's zoom. Bypass GeoGuessr auto-panning for locations with 0 heading. Keeps unpanned locations unpanned in the game. The format and options are remembered per map. CSV and GeoJSON CSV. Coordinates only ( lat,lng ): no views, pano IDs, tags, or metadata. For handing coordinates to another tool, not for moving a map. GeoJSON. For other mapping tools. Each point keeps its tag names. To export every map at once, use Export all maps on the home screen."},
	{"id":"syncing","title":"Syncing with a remote map or file","text":"Syncing keeps a map in step with a copy somewhere else. Importing copies once; syncing keeps going, carrying changes both ways. Three plugins do it: map-making.app sync. With a map on map-making.app. Needs an API key from map-making.app/keys . GeoGuessr. With one of your GeoGuessr maps. You sign in through a GeoGuessr window. It only changes the map's draft; publishing is still up to you on GeoGuessr. File sync. From a JSON or CSV file on your computer or at a URL (GitHub file links work). One way only: it reads the file and never writes to it. All three are experimental . Only link a map you have a backup of. File sync: follow a map file or URL. Linking Open the plugin from its button in the sidebar with your map open. Search your maps under Link this map and pick one, or give File sync a path or URL and press Link . With map-making.app or GeoGuessr you can also Create a new remote map from this one . If both sides already have locations, the first sync asks what to do: Merge. Keep everything from both. Nothing is deleted. Use remote. Make your map match theirs, deleting locations only you have. Use local. Make theirs match yours, deleting locations only they have. Not for File sync. After that Each sync sends your changes and brings in theirs. If both sides changed the same location, or one deleted what the other edited, you pick Keep local or Keep remote . Live. On by default. Syncs in the background while the map is open. Sync now. Syncs once. History. What each sync did. Unlink. Stops syncing. Neither map is deleted. A sync can be undone like any other edit. Linked maps show the provider's icon on the home screen."},
	{"id":"plugin-system","title":"Plugins","text":"Plugins add tools and panels to Map Making App. A plugin can add a sidebar panel, a full-window panel, or a section in the location editor. Some just add enrichment fields . The plugin marketplace: Core (built-in) and Additional (downloadable) tabs. The marketplace The puzzle-piece button in the bottom-right ( Ctrl + Shift + P ) has two tabs: Core . Plugins that come with Map Making App, each with an on/off switch. Additional . Plugins you can download, with buttons to install, update, and uninstall. A flask marks experimental plugins, and a wand marks ones that only add fields. A plugin that needs a newer Map Making App says so instead of installing. Using a plugin Turned-on plugins are there whenever a map is open. Open one from its button in the Selections header, or from the Plugins group in the command palette . Plugins that add a location editor section just show up there. Plugin setups are saved with the map: a generator's regions are still there next time you open that map. A plugin can do anything Map Making App can. Only install ones from people you trust."},
	{"id":"builtin-plugins","title":"Built-in plugins","text":"The plugins on the Core tab. Turn them on in the marketplace. Making locations Map generator . Finds Street View locations inside areas you draw. Vali . Finds locations from pre-built coverage data. Much faster, where the data exists. Understanding a map Distribution . How your locations are spread across countries. Disambiguate . Which metadata fields best tell your selections apart. Gradient . Colors locations by a field. Pivot Table . Counts locations across two groupings. Syncing map-making.app sync and GeoGuessr . Keep the map in step with one on those sites. File sync . Keeps the map up to date with a map file. Playing LocalGuessr . A guessing game on your own map."},
	{"id":"generating-locations","title":"Generating locations","text":"Two built-in plugins find locations for you instead of clicking them in one by one. What they find goes on the open map. Map generator The Map generator searches for Street View coverage inside areas you draw, and keeps what passes your filters. Draw a polygon selection first (or Q -click a country). The Map generator: draw regions, set coverage filters, and generate. Goal. Count aims for a number of locations; Spacing aims for a minimum gap between them. Sampling. With a count goal, where it looks: Random. Anywhere in the area. Uniform. Random, but spread apart. Grid. An even honeycomb over the whole area, in random order. Coverage. Along the blue lines themselves, weighted by road density, by area, or in between. Grow. Outward from locations already on the map. Filters. Camera generation, dates, official or unofficial, trekker, and more specific ones (number of links, bends in the road, words in the panorama description). Output. A tag for everything it finds, so you can find the batch later. You can change settings while it runs, and it keeps going while you do other things. Show search coverage draws where it's already looked. The import and export buttons at the top save the areas, settings, and output tag to one GeoJSON file, so you can reuse a setup later. Vali Vali finds locations from pre-built coverage data instead of searching Street View, so it's much faster where that data exists. The data for a country downloads the first time you need it. The download button at the top can also get a country, a continent, or everything ahead of time, and it's marked when your data is out of date. What comes out is ordinary locations. Thin them out , review them, and clean up duplicates like any others."},
	{"id":"analysis-plugins","title":"Analysis plugins","text":"Four built-in plugins tell you about a map instead of changing it. Most need the map enriched first. Distribution A bar chart of how many locations are in each country. It works out countries from coordinates, so it works without enrichment. Switch to Metadata to count by the enriched country code instead. The Distribution plugin: locations per country as a bar chart. Disambiguate Ranks metadata fields by how well they tell your selections apart. Select two tags you keep mixing up, and it shows which fields actually differ between them. Gradient Colors locations by a field: image year, altitude, time of day. It groups values the same ways as metadata as tags . It can color just the selection too. Pivot Table A table of counts with one grouping down the side and another across the top, each being selections or a metadata field. Countries against camera generations, for example. Ctrl -click a value to select those locations."},
	{"id":"localguessr","title":"LocalGuessr","text":"LocalGuessr is a GeoGuessr-style game on your own map. It's a quick way to play a map the way others will (or just for practice), and you can tag locations as you go. Setting up Turn it on in the marketplace, then open it with a map open: Locations. The whole map, or the selection. Mode. Moving , No move , or NMPZ . Rounds. A Fixed number, or Endless . Timer. None, counting up, or counting down. Streak. Optionally count a streak by country, or by state or region. Then press Play . Playing Look around, put a pin on the map, and press Guess (or Space ). You see the answer and your score; Space again moves on. When you can move, C sets a checkpoint, B goes back to it, and R goes back to the start. The tag bar on each round tags that location on your map, and Tag all rounds at the end tags them all. Games can be resumed, and Past games keeps your history and stats."},
	{"id":"installing-plugins","title":"Installing user plugins","text":"From the marketplace On the Additional tab, press install on a plugin. It downloads, turns itself on, and starts on the open map. Installed plugins update themselves when Map Making App starts; the trash button uninstalls. Some plugins come with a sidecar : a helper program for things the app can't do itself (image models, OCR). It downloads with the plugin, with a progress bar. Sidecars are bigger than the plugin, so the first install takes a while. By hand Plugins live in a plugins/ folder in your data folder , one folder each. To install one yourself: Copy the plugin's folder into plugins/ . Reopen the map. Turn it on in the marketplace."},
	{"id":"settings-overview","title":"App settings vs per-map settings","text":"Some settings apply everywhere. Others belong to one map, and changing them only changes that map. App settings, split into Street View, Map, Editing, Keyboard, Application, Integrations, Feedback, and Advanced sections. App settings The gear in the bottom-right ( Ctrl + , ). These apply to every map. The search box at the top searches every section at once, which is usually quicker than looking. Street View. Moving around, what the panorama shows, which controls are on it, fullscreen, and the date picker. Map. Defaults for new maps, pan speed, marker colors, panorama dots, borders, and fullscreen. Editing. Tags, seen history , and where addresses come from. Keyboard . Every shortcut. Application. Language, units, reopening maps on startup, what map rows show, updates, and the data folder . Integrations. Discord status, and a local API for controlling Map Making App from scripts. Feedback . Bug reports and ideas. Advanced. Custom CSS and debug tools. The Map settings dropdown The Map settings dropdown above the map has both kinds: Selecting new locations is per map. It's how a click picks coverage . Map behaviour and Display apply to every map: what a click does, hover previews, marker style and size, and the 5K radius. So does the Map style button. Edit map The pencil next to the map name opens Edit map , with the rest of the per-map settings: Name, description, and labels. Duplicate preference . Which duplicate is kept. Review order . Which locations a review shows first. Scoring . The score bounds. Enrichment settings are per map too, in their own dialog. So are plugin setups. Defaults for new maps If you set up every new map the same way, do it once and press Use current map in Settings → Map → Defaults . New maps start from that; existing ones keep theirs. Reset goes back to the built-in defaults."},
	{"id":"controls-hotkeys","title":"Controls & hotkeys","text":"Settings → Keyboard lists every shortcut, grouped by where it works: Commands, Global, Map Navigation, Location Editor, Quicktag, and Review. Every command in the command palette can be given a key, not just the built-in ones. It's also the full list of default keys. Changing a key Click a shortcut, then press the new keys. Backspace or Delete clears it, and Esc cancels. Filter the list with the search box. Reset puts one key back; Reset all to defaults puts them all back. Letter keys go by position on non-Latin keyboards, so the defaults still work. Conflicts If a key is already taken, Map Making App says by what and offers Reassign (which takes it off the other one). Conflicting rows are marked; click the mark to jump to the other one. Some keys aren't allowed: e.g. Alt combinations can't override movement keys, because Alt is the slow-down key. Per-map keys Some keys are set outside Settings, because they point at something that only exists on one map: Tag keys. Assign a key in a tag's edit dialog. Tags sharing a key toggle together: add any missing tags, or remove them all if every tag is present. These belong to the map. Copy-to-map keys. Copy location to map via hotkeys... in the command palette gives another map a key. Pressing it copies the open location into that map. Each one can work on this map only, or everywhere. Speeds Pan speed. How fast the keyboard moves the map ( Settings → Map → Navigation ). Pano look speed. How fast the keyboard turns the panorama ( Settings → Street View ). Alt slow-down. How much holding Alt slows things down ( Settings → Map → Navigation )."},
	{"id":"data-folder","title":"Data folder & diagnostics","text":"Map Making App keeps everything in one folder: your maps, tags, version history, seen history, and plugins. You never need to touch it, but it helps to know where it is. Windows: %APPDATA%\\app.map-making.local Linux: ~/.local/share/app.map-making.local macOS: ~/Library/Application Support/app.map-making.local Settings → Application → Data has: Open data folder. Opens it in your file browser. Change folder... Points Map Making App at a different folder (a bigger drive, or a synced one) and restarts. Your maps aren't moved for you, so copy them over first. Reset to default goes back to the normal folder. Back up before anything risky (bulk deletes, big imports, moving the data folder). The recommended backup is Export all maps on the home screen. Diagnostics Stats for nerds ( Ctrl + Shift + D ). Version, counts, storage size, and live performance numbers. The first thing to check when something feels slow. FPS counter , Open log file , and Copy diagnostics are in Settings → Advanced → Debug . Grab the log when reporting a bug, or let the feedback form attach it for you. Custom CSS in Settings → Advanced restyles anything in the app."},
	{"id":"feedback","title":"Feedback","text":"You can report a bug or suggest an idea from inside Map Making App, and read replies there too. Open Settings → Feedback and press Send feedback , or click Report bugs here on the home screen. The feedback form: what happened, what to attach, and a preview of exactly what will be sent. Sending a report Kind. Something is broken or Suggestion . Bug reports also have a Steps to reproduce box. Images. Attach screenshots by browsing, dragging them in, or pasting. Attachments. Your app version and plugins, the settings you've changed, and the recent log. On by default for bugs, off for suggestions. Preview. Show exactly what will be sent shows the whole report before you send it. Reports become public issues on the project's GitHub. Your username is removed from file paths, but don't include anything you wouldn't want public. You can report anonymously, or sign in with GitHub to file it under your account and get replies there too. Replies Your reports are listed in Settings → Feedback . A dot on the Settings gear means someone replied. Open a report to read and answer; Ctrl + Enter sends. Anonymous replies only come back to this computer."},
];
